From: liuqin Fedora maintainers <liuqin@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] soc: qcom: pmic_glink: register the rpmsg driver after platform probe

Source: Linaro arm64-laptops hack 46a6b15977c0af848516f5ccc771908d28ffc766
("soc: qcom: pmic_glink: Avoid platform/rpmsg probe race condition"), same
workaround carried as 627df39829912f7e55e0a4ceef7aa21e12ca229e in radxa-kernel.
Problem: PMIC_RTR_ADSP_APPS can appear while the pmic-glink platform device is
still deferred on pd-mapper. rpmsg_dev_probe() then returns -ENODEV and the
rpmsg core destroys the endpoint without retrying, leaving pg->ept NULL; clients
never receive service-up and every qcom-battmgr property returns -EAGAIN.
Reproduced on liuqin 2026-11-15: endpoint at 5.325 s, platform device at
13.041 s, battery/Type-C sysfs unreadable for the whole boot.
Upstream's proposed -EPROBE_DEFER change (Konrad Dybcio, 2026-07-20,
https://lore-kernel.gnuweeb.org/lkml/20260720-topic-pmic_glink_defer-v1-1-94c0f233f2f7@oss.qualcomm.com/)
does not help here because rpmsg_dev_probe() destroys the endpoint on any
probe error and does not retry.
Adaptation: keep module init limited to the platform driver and register the
rpmsg driver at the end of a successful platform probe; an endpoint that
already exists is bound immediately, and one that appears later finds the
platform side ready. Unregister it first in remove.
Exit condition: drop once the rpmsg core handles deferred probes or upstream
provides an equivalent ordering guarantee.
Validation and rollback: tasks/upstream-refresh/RESULT.md.

---
--- a/drivers/soc/qcom/pmic_glink.c
+++ b/drivers/soc/qcom/pmic_glink.c
@@ -554,6 +554,21 @@
 	__pmic_glink = pg;
 	mutex_unlock(&__pmic_glink_lock);
 
+	/*
+	 * rpmsg_dev_probe() destroys the endpoint when probe returns any error,
+	 * and the PMIC_RTR_ADSP_APPS endpoint may appear before this platform
+	 * device gets past its pd-mapper deferral. Register the rpmsg driver
+	 * only now: an endpoint that already exists is bound here, and one that
+	 * appears later finds the platform side ready.
+	 */
+	ret = register_rpmsg_driver(&pmic_glink_rpmsg_driver);
+	if (ret) {
+		mutex_lock(&__pmic_glink_lock);
+		__pmic_glink = NULL;
+		mutex_unlock(&__pmic_glink_lock);
+		goto out_release_aux_devices;
+	}
+
 	return 0;
 
 out_release_aux_devices:
@@ -575,6 +590,9 @@
 {
 	struct pmic_glink *pg = dev_get_drvdata(&pdev->dev);
 
+	/* Drop the endpoint before clients and the GLINK service go away. */
+	unregister_rpmsg_driver(&pmic_glink_rpmsg_driver);
+
 	cancel_work_sync(&pg->deliver_work);
 	pdr_handle_release(pg->pdr);
 
@@ -648,25 +666,13 @@
 
 static int pmic_glink_init(void)
 {
-	int ret;
-
-	ret = platform_driver_register(&pmic_glink_driver);
-	if (ret < 0)
-		return ret;
-
-	ret = register_rpmsg_driver(&pmic_glink_rpmsg_driver);
-	if (ret < 0) {
-		platform_driver_unregister(&pmic_glink_driver);
-		return ret;
-	}
-
-	return 0;
+	/* The rpmsg driver is registered from a successful platform probe. */
+	return platform_driver_register(&pmic_glink_driver);
 }
 module_init(pmic_glink_init);
 
 static void pmic_glink_exit(void)
 {
-	unregister_rpmsg_driver(&pmic_glink_rpmsg_driver);
 	platform_driver_unregister(&pmic_glink_driver);
 }
 module_exit(pmic_glink_exit);
